hey knew about the  person based on all the other data that  they had about them so that's why when  you look for something let's say a type  of shoe one day on  one device that adds for similar pairs  of shoes may be through different  vendors are gonna follow you around the  internet across different browsers and  different devices for any number of days  now there are there's a wide spectrum of  sort of opinion as to whether this is a  good thing a bad thing an immoral thing  an immoral thing which would be a sort  of different different assessment but a  common refrain that I hear not only from  people who are working in the industry  but also from people who sort of claim  to just be objective analyzers of modern  advertising they say there's going to be  advertising people aren't willing to pay  for all content on the internet and the  way that they can pay for it without  paying for it with cash is by looking at  ads or being subjected to ads isn't  everybody better off if the ads are  targeted to the interests of the  individual that's the the kind of  talking point that is a common refrain  how would we start to or might we start  to break that apart into its component  pieces to consider whether it's actually  such a win-win right so that's a really  good point and I think it's likely that  that advertising in some form is is here  to stay as you say there's somebody has  to pay for for content to be produced  and it does seem that the market isn't  going to support the full cost of  producing data or you know things that  you read things that you watch think  other kinds of entertainment or  information online at the same time we  have to think really carefully about  what it is they were giving up when  we're surrendering our privacy by  agreeing that anyone who has the  technical capacity to can track whatever  you do on the Internet  combine that with any other data sources  that they can acquire about you from  credit card companies banks your cell  phone provider your ISP anywhere else  and just say that you can do whatever  you want within that debt with that data  as long as you can make it happen  right I think there have to be some kind  of limits so for example there's  story yesterday that came out in the  verge about how several cellphone  companies in the US including t-mobile  were actually selling really really  detailed location data about their  customers to all kinds of data  aggregators and who were then able to  recreate exactly what people do day to  day where they go what they do when  they're there and create like these  whole patterns of life to use kind of a  spy jargon to figure out what everybody  is doing and then figure out exactly how  to insert specific ads into that  person's day in a way that feels really  creepy and invasive to a lot of people  so I think it's you have to think  carefully about what kind of data is  allowed to be collected about people in  what circumstances and and I think in  that case consent is really important  because most of this collection happens  without people even knowing about it  right so yeah along those lines  can you talk a little bit about the  difference between opting in and opting  out when it comes to this type of  information gathering right so when  you're opting in it means that by  default people can do can it can assume  that they are not being tracked unless a  notification comes up prominently and  says do you consent to being trapped for  this or do you or do you consent to  having this data about you collected in  this context and this is why we this is  why we the the website for example once  and what we might use it for and I think  for the most part that's completely fine  but then there's the assumption of  opt-out which is that you have to assume  that you're being tracked at all times  and that maybe if you dig deep enough  you can find a setting to opt out of  that but a lot of times that's only  partially effective because every time  you come back to the site or you reboot  the app or what have you you have to  opt-out again so it's it's one of those  things where it's become if the  companies we're tracking you are trying  so hard to do it without your knowledge  isn't that a little fishy well let's  explore that because you know I think  that we could have a reasonable  disagreement as to where the line should  be when it comes to what information  should or shouldn't be opt in opt out  nope no question that that's a  legitimate conversation but where I want  to go with you next is that this is not  just about products or services or web  hosting or sneakers or whatever the case  may be we saw how the same data that a  sneaker company can use to target people  on facebook was used by foreign powers  to try to influence the 2016 election as  one example can you talk a little bit  about the non sort of innocuous let's  target clothing and grocery order  services to people and talk about the  ways in which authoritarianism in  different places around the world is  sort of fueled by this technology that's  right so what's really important to  understand is that this digital  infrastructure that makes target  advertising possible is at its core an  infrastructure of influence right the  point of advertising is to influence  people to do something or not do  something and that same infrastructure  can be used just as easily to sell  sneakers as you say as it can to sell  white supremacy to propagate hate speech  to propagate disinformation and this can  beat us for information like giving  incorrect facts to minority and  low-income voters to tell them that  Election Day is actually on Wednesday  rather than Tuesday we saw that  happening in the last election in the US  it can be spreading lies about entire  ethnic groups and encouraging people to  harm them and that's something that's  been going on in Myanmar notably via  Facebook and it seems to me that it's  very difficult to to draw a 