om the person whose  information we're talking about and then  maintaining the right for for that  person to find out what information is  held about them have it corrected if  it's wrong or even asked for have it  deleted and make it very clear that  people maintain  in the right to control the information  that circulates about them throughout  this this infrastructure of influence  that that exists outside of our eyesight  is this politically viable in the sense  not from what it is that the end-user  would prefer or tolerate but just sort  of pragmatically thinking about the  influence that the companies that are  doing this have on our elected officials  both Democratic and Republican you know  if there's anything I've learned that  past two years is to not make any not  make any guesses about what's going to  happen next in the world of American  politics fair enough no but I guess I  mean so aside from predicting what would  happen just having an understanding of  the way in which companies like Google  and Facebook and Apple are so much a  part of the conversation around how they  should be regulated without making any  prediction would you grant that the  infrastructure that's in place is one  that is not particularly favorable to  our elected officials merely making  policy that reflects the desire of the  people and that the lobbying dot-com  industrial complex is one that is  powerful I think we would accept I mean  I think I think your analysis is is  accurate for sure but at the same time I  think there's a great danger here in  giving up the fight before we even start  it yes and just accepting certain things  as inevitable without actually trying to  change them yeah I mean I think if  anything what the last you know several  decades 200 years of activism have told  us is if you concede as inevitable  something you've definitely lost and if  you don't then at least it's a  conversation that you can fight over  that's right we've been speaking with  dr. Natalie Marichal who is a senior  research analyst at ranking digital  rights Natalie thank you so much for  talking to me today about this my  pleasure thanks for having me

